.TH CCOL_R_MEMPOOL_CREATE 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_r_mempool_create \- create a ranged memory pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

ccol_r_mempool *ccol_r_mempool_create(
    uint8_t smallest_size_power_of_two,
    uint8_t largest_size_power_of_two,
    uint8_t number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two,
    ccol_r_memory_fallback_policy_t fb_policy,
    bool single_threaded,
    ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs,
    char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a ranged memory pool that manages multiple fixed-size pools
organized by power-of-2 sizes. Automatically selects the appropriate
sub-pool for each allocation request.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
smallest_size_power_of_two              Exponent for smallest pool size
largest_size_power_of_two               Exponent for largest pool size
number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two  Exponent for smallest pool count
fb_policy                               Fallback policy for exhausted pools
single_threaded                         If true, omit locking
mmgmt_procs                             Custom memory management, or NULL
err                                     Optional pointer to receive error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to newly created ranged memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Each sub-pool has half the elements of the previous size
Minimum allowed smallest size is 16 bytes (power = 4)
Example: smallest=4 (16 bytes), largest=6 (64 bytes), count=8 (256 elems)
  Creates pools: 16 bytes (256 elems), 32 bytes (128), 64 bytes (64)
Fallback policies:
  - ccol_fallback_disabled: Returns NULL when pools exhausted
  - ccol_fallback_at_first_exhaustion: Uses heap when first pool exhausted
  - ccol_fallback_at_last_exhaustion: Uses heap only after all pools exhausted

Sizing for performance:

Each sub-pool is sized independently, so the 8-element floor described in
ccol_mempool_create(3) applies per tier rather than to the pool as a
whole. Because each tier holds half the elements of the one below it, the
largest few element sizes have the fewest entries and are the first to
fall under that floor: a tier with fewer than 8 elements takes its lock
on every allocation and every free.

Raising number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two lifts every tier at
once and is usually the simplest way to keep the large-element tiers
above the floor. Narrowing the range between smallest_size_power_of_two
and largest_size_power_of_two has the same effect, by producing fewer
tiers to divide the elements between.

Every tier that receives a cache allocates a reserve of its own, so a
ranged pool's memory can exceed the sum of its nominal tier sizes by up
to half, and by proportionally less for tiers large enough that their
caches reach maximum depth. That memory is allocated when the pool is
created, so nothing grows at run time.
ccol_r_mempool_total_capacity() reports a tier's nominal count, and a
tier escalates or falls back once that count is exhausted. The exception
is the same one ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3) describes: an entry leaves
a thread's cache with no lock held and so with no count to consult, so
concurrent callers can briefly hold a few entries more than a tier's
nominal count at once, never more than that count plus the tier's own
reserve.

The best range, tier count and single_threaded setting depend on the size
distribution of the program's own allocations, which no default can
predict. Measure rather than assume: "make bench" in the source tree runs
these pools alongside the system allocator on the current machine.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when single_threaded is false. Each sub-pool carries its own
lock, so allocations for different size classes never contend with one
another, and within a tier large enough to maintain per-thread caches the
common path takes no lock at all.

Entries held in a thread's cache return to their tier when that thread
exits or when the pool is destroyed. Join a thread before destroying a
pool it used.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_r_mempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_total_capacity (3)
